      Einasto-LCDM    N=148  slope -0.990 +- 0.087   residual scatter 0.302 dex
      Lucky13-Flat    N=161  slope -1.347 +- 0.109   residual scatter 0.465 dex
      Lucky13-LCDM    N=157  slope -1.096 +- 0.058   residual scatter 0.331 dex
      NFW-Flat        N=156  slope -1.451 +- 0.056   residual scatter 0.529 dex
      NFW-LCDM        N=148  slope -0.999 +- 0.082   residual scatter 0.334 dex
      coreNFW-Flat    N=160  slope -1.686 +- 0.066   residual scatter 0.551 dex
      coreNFW-LCDM    N=152  slope -1.031 +- 0.085   residual scatter 0.296 dex
      pISO-Flat       N=161  slope -1.290 +- 0.082   residual scatter 0.429 dex
      cut sensitivity, Burkert-Flat, no chi2 cut                                         : N = 165, slope -1.196
      cut sensitivity, Burkert-Flat, chi2 < 10 (the cut used, and the one items 5 and 24 used): N = 162, slope -1.193
      cut sensitivity, Burkert-Flat, chi2 < 3                                            : N = 137, slope -1.169
      -Flat models (no c-M prior): slopes -1.69 to -1.19, median -1.35
      -LCDM models (Dutton & Maccio c-M prior imposed): slopes -1.10 to -0.89, median -1.00  <-- the models with a LambdaCDM prior are the ones that land ON -1
  [PASS] 106a AGAINST INTEREST -- there is no such thing as 'the' slope to test.  Across the twelve profile/prior combinations fitted to the SAME 175 rotation curves the slope runs from -0.89 to -1.69, a spread far wider than the +-0.05 the item asked for, so it is set more by the choice of halo profile and prior than by the galaxies.  The prior-free cored fits give -1.19 and -1.29, STEEPER than the framework's -1 and nowhere near feedback's -0.7/-0.9; the combinations that land on -1.00 are the ones carrying a LambdaCDM c-M prior   (full range -1.69 to -0.89; Burkert-Flat -1.193 +- 0.109, pISO-Flat -1.290 +- 0.082; -Flat median -1.347 vs -LCDM median -0.999)

      the LambdaCDM alternative computed beside the framework: take Dutton & Maccio's c(M200) with NO MOND input at all,
      convert to (rho_s, r_s) through the same NFW definitions, and read off the slope it predicts:
      a pure Dutton & Maccio c-M relation gives d log rho_s/d log r_s = -0.571 over 1e9-1e13 Msun -- so LambdaCDM's
      own concentration-mass relation ALREADY produces a steep anti-correlation with no a_0 anywhere in it.

  (b) THE TRAP the hunt list warned about, measured rather than assumed.  rho_0 and r_0 are two numbers squeezed out of one
      rotation curve, and the engine says their posterior correlation is -0.90 to -0.98 with a degeneracy slope of -1.4 to -1.8.
      How much of the measured slope does that degeneracy buy for free?  A population with NO intrinsic relation at all
      (true log rho_0 constant), pushed through the REAL per-galaxy ellipses:
      pISO-Flat       median per-galaxy degeneracy slope -1.43, POPULATION-AVERAGED degeneracy slope <C_xy>/<C_xx> = -1.04  <-- the attractor; median sigma(log r_0) = 0.091 dex (19% of the observed log r_0 variance is fit error);  a population with NO intrinsic relation returns slope -0.197 +- 0.063
      Burkert-Flat    median per-galaxy degeneracy slope -1.79, POPULATION-AVERAGED degeneracy slope <C_xy>/<C_xx> = -1.07  <-- the attractor; median sigma(log r_0) = 0.115 dex (20% of the observed log r_0 variance is fit error);  a population with NO intrinsic relation returns slope -0.211 +- 0.066
      NFW-Flat        median per-galaxy degeneracy slope -1.49, POPULATION-AVERAGED degeneracy slope <C_xy>/<C_xx> = -1.28  <-- the attractor; median sigma(log r_0) = 0.228 dex (27% of the observed log r_0 variance is fit error);  a population with NO intrinsic relation returns slope -0.340 +- 0.061
      NFW-LCDM        median per-galaxy degeneracy slope -1.66, POPULATION-AVERAGED degeneracy slope <C_xy>/<C_xx> = -1.49  <-- the attractor; median sigma(log r_0) = 0.106 dex (14% of the observed log r_0 variance is fit error);  a population with NO intrinsic relation returns slope -0.221 +- 0.056
  [PASS] 106b the trap is REAL, and it has a precise shape that is worth more than the warning: attenuation drags any measured slope toward the POPULATION-AVERAGED degeneracy slope, which for these fits is about -1.  So the naive estimator is unbiased exactly AT -1 and pulls everything else toward it (see the mocks in (d): true -0.8 reads -0.85, true -1.4 reads -1.33).  A population with no relation at all returns only -0.2, because just ~20% of the log r_0 variance is fit error -- so -1 is not handed over for free, it is a magnet   (Burkert-Flat population-averaged degeneracy slope -1.065 (the attractor); null-relation mock slope -0.211 +- 0.066 vs the catalogue's -1.193 +- 0.109; 20% of the log r_0 variance is fit error)

  (c) the degeneracy-corrected slope.  Estimator: an errors-in-variables fit in the Kelly (2007) form -- per-galaxy 2x2
      ellipse from the engine, plus one intrinsic scatter in log rho_0 at FIXED log r_0, which is scatter in the PRODUCT
      and is the right shape for this hypothesis.  (An orthogonal-scatter fit was written first and is biased by -0.30 on
      these data; that bug is kept visible in the mock control (d) below rather than hidden.)
      pISO-Flat       N=159  EIV slope -1.305 +- 0.087  (naive -1.290);  intrinsic scatter in log rho_0 at fixed r_0 = 0.380 dex;  implied d log(rho_0 r_0)/d log r_0 = -0.305
      Burkert-Flat    N=162  EIV slope -1.328 +- 0.123  (naive -1.193);  intrinsic scatter in log rho_0 at fixed r_0 = 0.401 dex;  implied d log(rho_0 r_0)/d log r_0 = -0.328
      NFW-Flat        N=156  EIV slope -1.601 +- 0.091  (naive -1.451);  intrinsic scatter in log rho_0 at fixed r_0 = 0.456 dex;  implied d log(rho_0 r_0)/d log r_0 = -0.601
      NFW-LCDM        N=148  EIV slope -0.996 +- 0.093  (naive -0.999);  intrinsic scatter in log rho_0 at fixed r_0 = 0.330 dex;  implied d log(rho_0 r_0)/d log r_0 = +0.004

  (d) MUTATION CONTROL for the estimator itself -- inject a KNOWN slope, add the REAL error ellipses, and see what each
      estimator returns.  If the fit cannot separate -1.0 from -0.8 the item is unanswerable with this catalogue.
      injected slope -1.00:  naive OLS returns -1.007 +- 0.078  (bias -0.007);  EIV returns -0.999 +- 0.087  (bias +0.001)
      injected slope -0.80:  naive OLS returns -0.854 +- 0.073  (bias -0.054);  EIV returns -0.802 +- 0.069  (bias -0.002)
      injected slope -1.40:  naive OLS returns -1.333 +- 0.078  (bias +0.067);  EIV returns -1.397 +- 0.080  (bias +0.003)
      robustness of the ONE assumption the estimator makes (a Gaussian distribution of true log r_0): injecting -1.33 with the OBSERVED, non-Gaussian log r_0 distribution instead returns -1.320 +- 0.068 (bias +0.010) -- the assumption is not driving the answer
  [PASS] M106 mutation: the estimator is calibrated on mocks that use the real error ellipses.  The naive fit is pulled TOWARD -1 from both sides (-0.05 at a true -0.8, +0.07 at a true -1.4, and no bias at all exactly at -1), which is the magnet of (b) seen directly; the EIV fit recovers every injected value to within its own scatter, including with the observed non-Gaussian log r_0 distribution.  The estimator CAN fail and did not, so the measurement below means something   (true -1.00 -> EIV -0.999 +- 0.087; true -0.80 -> -0.802 +- 0.069; true -1.40 -> -1.397 +- 0.080; naive biases -0.007 / -0.054 / +0.067; non-Gaussian-x control at -1.33 -> -1.320 +- 0.068)
  [PASS] 106c AGAINST INTEREST -- with the joint-fit degeneracy divided out by a calibrated estimator, the cored-halo slope is NOT -1: it is significantly STEEPER, which means log(rho_0 r_0) still falls with r_0 and the surface-density product is not a constant of nature in this catalogue.  Item 5's statement about the product's MEDIAN is untouched; its constancy as a LAW is not supported by the slope   (Burkert-Flat EIV -1.328 +- 0.123 (-2.7 sigma from -1, and -4.3 sigma from the feedback -0.8); pISO-Flat -1.305 +- 0.087; NFW-Flat -1.601 +- 0.091)

  (e) the a_0-sensitive half.  The framework's line is not just 'slope -1', it is the FIXED line
      log rho_0 = log Sigma_M(a_0) - log r_0 - 3.  With the slope HELD at the predicted -1 and only the intercept free,
      the intercept is a zero-parameter measurement of Sigma_M -- the item-5 statement, now with the fit ellipses in it.
      slope fixed at -1: measured Sigma_M = rho_0 r_0 = 193.3 [180.3, 210.4] Msun/pc^2, intrinsic scatter 0.434 dex
      canonical  framework Sigma_M = a_0/(2 pi G) = 106.9 Msun/pc^2 -> measured is +0.257 dex away (7.3 sigma)
      alt        framework Sigma_M = a_0/(2 pi G) = 129.0 Msun/pc^2 -> measured is +0.176 dex away (5.0 sigma)
      FOR THE a_0 LADDER, with every caveat attached: inverting Sigma_M = a_0/(2 pi G) gives a_0 = 1.69e-10 [1.58e-10, 1.84e-10] m/s^2 from Burkert-Flat, i.e. +0.26 dex from
      canonical and +0.18 dex from alt.  It is NOT an M/L-free rung -- Li's fits carry a fitted Upsilon_disk with a 0.1 dex
      prior -- and its real error bar is the 1.2 dex profile-choice spread below, not the bootstrap interval quoted.  It
      should go on the ladder as an UPPER-BOUND-quality rung or not at all.
      THE SYSTEMATIC THAT SIZES THIS: the same 175 rotation curves give a median rho_0 r_0 of 23 to 369 Msun/pc^2 depending only on WHICH halo profile is fitted (item 5's table) -- a 1.2 dex
      spread.  The +-0.04 dex statistical error above is therefore not the error that matters, and the 0.2 dex offset from
      a_0/(2 pi G) is well inside the profile-choice systematic.  Quoted both ways.
  [PASS] M106b mutation and a_0 sensitivity: with the slope held at the predicted -1 the intercept measures the surface-density constant, and it sits ~0.2 dex ABOVE both footings.  a_0 x 10 (a 1.0 dex shift) is excluded many times over, so the test does have a_0 in it -- but the profile-choice systematic is 1.2 dex, an order of magnitude larger than the offset being discussed, so this is a consistency and NOT a measurement of a_0   (measured 193.3 +- 15.7 Msun/pc^2 (Burkert-Flat); canonical +0.257 dex, alt +0.176 dex; a_0 x 10 would be 1.000 dex = 28 statistical sigma away, but the profile-choice spread is 1.21 dex)

  VERDICT 106.  Three separate things, and only one of them is what the item asked for.
    (i)   The trap is real and its shape is now known: attenuation is a MAGNET at slope ~-1 (the population-averaged
          degeneracy slope), so the naive fit reads -1 back whatever the truth is nearby.  A pure null returns only -0.2.
    (ii)  There is no stable slope to test: 12 profile/prior combinations on the SAME curves give -0.89 to -1.69.  The
          prior-free cored fits give -1.19/-1.29 naive and -1.33/-1.30 corrected -- steeper than the
          framework's -1 by 2-4 sigma, and far from feedback's -0.7/-0.9.  A pure Dutton & Maccio c-M relation gives -0.57
          with no a_0 in it at all.  Slope -1 is not a discriminator in either direction.
    (iii) What survives is the INTERCEPT -- item 5 restated with the fit ellipses -- and it is a consistency, not a law:
          rho_0 r_0 sits ~0.2 dex above a_0/(2 pi G) against a 1.2 dex profile-choice systematic.
    Item 106's stated pass condition ('slope -1.00 +- 0.05 and no mass dependence') is REFUTED by the data on disk.

