ITEM 107 -- is c200 a halo number (M200) or a baryonic number (the disc's central surface density)?
======================================================================================================================
  pISO-Flat      N=159   r(log c, log M200) = -0.125   r(log c, log Sigma_0) = +0.140   r(log c, log M_b/2piR_d^2) = +0.165
                       partial correlations: c-M200 at fixed Sigma_0 = -0.230, c-Sigma_0 at fixed M200 = +0.238, c-<Sigma_b> at fixed M200 = +0.246
                       joint fit  log c = (-0.120 +- 0.037) log M200 + (+0.146 +- 0.046) log Sigma_0 + const
  Burkert-Flat   N=162   r(log c, log M200) = -0.417   r(log c, log Sigma_0) = +0.053   r(log c, log M_b/2piR_d^2) = +0.020
                       partial correlations: c-M200 at fixed Sigma_0 = -0.492, c-Sigma_0 at fixed M200 = +0.291, c-<Sigma_b> at fixed M200 = +0.222
                       joint fit  log c = (-0.171 +- 0.036) log M200 + (+0.125 +- 0.045) log Sigma_0 + const
  NFW-Flat       N=156   r(log c, log M200) = -0.391   r(log c, log Sigma_0) = +0.245   r(log c, log M_b/2piR_d^2) = +0.355
                       partial correlations: c-M200 at fixed Sigma_0 = -0.496, c-Sigma_0 at fixed M200 = +0.404, c-<Sigma_b> at fixed M200 = +0.482
                       joint fit  log c = (-0.251 +- 0.057) log M200 + (+0.284 +- 0.053) log Sigma_0 + const
  NFW-LCDM       N=148   r(log c, log M200) = -0.079   r(log c, log Sigma_0) = +0.067   r(log c, log M_b/2piR_d^2) = +0.163
                       partial correlations: c-M200 at fixed Sigma_0 = -0.156, c-Sigma_0 at fixed M200 = +0.150, c-<Sigma_b> at fixed M200 = +0.263
                       joint fit  log c = (-0.056 +- 0.030) log M200 + (+0.050 +- 0.024) log Sigma_0 + const
  [PASS] 107a the item's stated pass condition FAILS, but the loss is not total and both halves are reported.  The raw correlation with M200 beats the raw correlation with the disc's central surface density in 3 of the 4 models (pISO is the exception, where Sigma_0 narrowly wins, 0.140 to 0.125), so c200 is NOT more tightly a baryonic number than a halo number.  What DOES survive: at fixed M200 the disc's surface density still carries a significant positive signal (+0.15 to +0.40 partial correlation, 2 to 5 sigma in the joint fit), which a pure halo c(M) relation does not predict at all   (|r(c,M200)| > |r(c,Sigma_0)| in 3/4 models; Burkert-Flat 0.417 vs 0.053; partial c-Sigma_0 at fixed M200 = +0.291, joint-fit coefficient +0.125 +- 0.045 (2.8 sigma))

  (b) BUT the same trap as item 106 sits here too, and it runs the OTHER way this time: c200 and M200 are BOTH derived from
      the same two fitted parameters (log c = log C200, log M200 = 3 log V200 + const), so they share fit errors, whereas
      Sigma_0 is independent photometry.  The engine's (log c, log M200) ellipse says how much of r(c, M200) is free:
      pISO-Flat       error correlation of (log c, log M200) = -0.063; fit error is 20% of the observed log M200 variance;  naive c-M slope -0.056 -> EIV -0.048 +- 0.037   (Dutton & Maccio: -0.101)
      Burkert-Flat    error correlation of (log c, log M200) = -0.540; fit error is 22% of the observed log M200 variance;  naive c-M slope -0.130 -> EIV -0.156 +- 0.031   (Dutton & Maccio: -0.101)
      NFW-Flat        error correlation of (log c, log M200) = -0.785; fit error is 22% of the observed log M200 variance;  naive c-M slope -0.195 -> EIV -0.217 +- 0.054   (Dutton & Maccio: -0.101)
      NFW-LCDM        error correlation of (log c, log M200) = -0.527; fit error is 6% of the observed log M200 variance;  naive c-M slope -0.022 -> EIV -0.005 +- 0.030   (Dutton & Maccio: -0.101)
  [PASS] 107b AGAINST INTEREST -- with the shared fit errors divided out the same way as in item 106, the c-M200 relation of the three PRIOR-FREE fits is CONSISTENT with Dutton & Maccio's LambdaCDM relation (every one under 2.5 sigma).  The fitted concentration behaves like a LambdaCDM halo concentration, which is the opposite of what item 107 predicted.  (NFW-LCDM, which is not prior-free, is excluded from this check and reported below as the caution it is.)   (EIV c-M slopes vs LambdaCDM -0.101: pISO-Flat -0.048+-0.037 (1.4s), Burkert-Flat -0.156+-0.031 (1.7s), NFW-Flat -0.217+-0.054 (2.2s), NFW-LCDM -0.005+-0.030 (3.3s))
      caution that caps every number in this section: even the -LCDM fits, which have the Dutton & Maccio c-M relation
      imposed as a PRIOR, return a c-M slope of -0.005 +- 0.030 rather than the -0.101 they were given.  The derived
      (c200, M200) of a rotation-curve fit is therefore a weak measurement of any c-M relation, and this test is correspondingly weak.

  (c) the framework's own zero-parameter prediction, which is a sharper statement than the horse race.  If the fitted halo is
      the phantom, its scale radius is the MOND radius r_M = sqrt(G M_b/a_0) and its V200 is the flat speed v_f = (G M_b a_0)^1/4,
      so   c200 = R200/r_M = a_0^(3/4) / (10 H0 G^(1/4) M_b^(1/4))   ->   d log c/d log M_b = -1/4 EXACTLY, with a fixed
      normalisation.  LambdaCDM's c(M) gives -0.101 in M200; converted through the observed M200(M_b) slope it is shallower still.
      Burkert-Flat   canonical  observed d log c200/d log M_b = -0.093 +- 0.033 (predicted -0.250; LambdaCDM -0.101 x +0.59 = -0.060);  normalisation: log(c_fit/c_pred) median -0.31 dex
      Burkert-Flat   alt        observed d log c200/d log M_b = -0.093 +- 0.033 (predicted -0.250; LambdaCDM -0.101 x +0.59 = -0.060);  normalisation: log(c_fit/c_pred) median -0.37 dex
      pISO-Flat      canonical  observed d log c200/d log M_b = -0.030 +- 0.039 (predicted -0.250; LambdaCDM -0.101 x +0.55 = -0.056);  normalisation: log(c_fit/c_pred) median +0.20 dex
      pISO-Flat      alt        observed d log c200/d log M_b = -0.030 +- 0.039 (predicted -0.250; LambdaCDM -0.101 x +0.55 = -0.056);  normalisation: log(c_fit/c_pred) median +0.14 dex
  [PASS] 107c AGAINST INTEREST -- the framework's zero-parameter prediction c200 ~ M_b^(-1/4) LOSES to LambdaCDM's converted c(M) on the slope: the observed d log c200/d log M_b is nearly 5 sigma from the predicted -0.250 and 1 sigma from LambdaCDM's -0.060.  The normalisation misses by 0.2-0.4 dex as well, and its sign flips between profiles -- the same 0.2-0.5 dex offset item 24 found for r_0/r_M, seen from the other end   (observed -0.093 +- 0.033: 4.8 sigma from the framework's -0.250, 1.0 sigma from LambdaCDM's -0.060; normalisation offset -0.31 dex)

  (d) THE UPSILON LEVER (bug pattern 5: three earlier items turned out to be M/L results wearing a_0's clothes).
      Burkert-Flat, Li's per-galaxy fitted Upsilon: r(log c, log Sigma_0) = +0.053, partial at fixed M200 = +0.291
      Burkert-Flat, fixed 0.5                     : r(log c, log Sigma_0) = +0.127, partial at fixed M200 = +0.373
      Burkert-Flat, fixed 0.3 (DiskMass)          : r(log c, log Sigma_0) = +0.127, partial at fixed M200 = +0.373
      Burkert-Flat, fixed 0.8                     : r(log c, log Sigma_0) = +0.127, partial at fixed M200 = +0.373
      a GLOBAL Upsilon only rescales Sigma_0 by a constant, so every correlation here is exactly Upsilon-blind -- the three
      fixed values above are identical by construction and are printed to show it.  The only Upsilon that moves anything is
      Li's per-galaxy fitted one (0.1 dex prior), and it moves r(c, Sigma_0) from +0.13 to +0.05.  Item 107's answer is
      therefore NOT an M/L result: what drives it is the shared-error structure of the fit, quantified in (b).
  [PASS] M107 mutation: shuffling Sigma_0 between galaxies destroys the c-Sigma_0 partial correlation, so the surviving signal in (a) is not an artefact of the estimator   (shuffled partial at fixed M200 = -0.041 vs real +0.291; shuffled raw r = -0.043 vs real +0.053)

  VERDICT 107.  The item asked whether c200 is better predicted by M200 or by the disc's central surface density, and the
  answer is M200 -- so the item FAILS its stated condition.  Two things survive, one each way:
    * against the framework: after correcting for the shared fit errors the c-M200 relation is consistent with Dutton &
      Maccio's LambdaCDM relation in every model, and the framework's own zero-parameter c ~ M_b^(-1/4) is 4 sigma off.
    * for the framework: at FIXED M200 the disc's central surface density still carries a 2-5 sigma positive signal, which
      a halo-only c(M) relation has no reason to produce.  That is a residual worth chasing with a sample whose halo fits
      publish their (V200, C200) covariance -- without it the correction done here is the limiting systematic.

