  [PASS] 108b the ratio is NOT one number: on the subsample where the switch-on radius exists at all, r_0/r(g_bar = a_0) has 0.36-0.72 dex of scatter against the 0.2 dex the item asked for, and its 0.03 dex edge over the disc scale length -- the feedback/LambdaCDM comparison -- is nothing.  Item 108 FAILS its own bar, both footings and every cored profile   (Burkert-Flat canonical: median 1.56, scatter 0.358 dex (bar was 0.2), mass slope +0.066; r_0/R_disk scatter 0.393; alt footing scatter 0.401)
  [PASS] 108c and the comparison is a dead heat: NONE of the four candidate length scales -- the switch-on radius, the MOND radius, the disc scale length, the effective radius -- gets the fitted core radius below 0.33 dex of scatter.  Two of them contain a_0 and two do not, and they are separated by less than 0.06 dex.  There is no length scale in this catalogue that predicts the fitted core   (Burkert-Flat canonical: r_0/r(g=a_0) 0.358, r_0/r_M 0.340, r_0/R_disk 0.393, r_0/R_eff 0.393 dex; tightest is r_M, spread between them 0.054 dex)

  (the UPSILON lever for item 108: r(g_bar = a_0) moves with Upsilon because g_bar does.  Recomputed with a global Upsilon:)
      Upsilon = 0.3: 127/175 = 73% of SPARC still never reaches a_0 anywhere;  on the rest, r_0/r(g_bar=a_0) median 2.02, scatter 0.417 dex (N = 46)
      Upsilon = 0.5: 114/175 = 65% of SPARC still never reaches a_0 anywhere;  on the rest, r_0/r(g_bar=a_0) median 1.34, scatter 0.437 dex (N = 58)
      Upsilon = 0.8: 106/175 = 61% of SPARC still never reaches a_0 anywhere;  on the rest, r_0/r(g_bar=a_0) median 1.25, scatter 0.453 dex (N = 66)
      read both ways: the SCATTER, which is what item 108's bar is on, is Upsilon-robust (0.42 to 0.45 dex over the whole
      plausible range, never near 0.2), and so is the 'no switch-on radius' finding (61-73%).  But the MEDIAN ratio moves
      0.21 dex between Upsilon = 0.3 and 0.8, so the ratio's NORMALISATION is an M/L-sensitive number and is not quoted as
      anything else.  The verdict rests on the scatter and on the premise, neither of which moves with Upsilon.
      ONE caveat stated against interest: r_M = sqrt(G M_b/a_0) uses the TOTAL baryonic mass, and for the smaller galaxies
      r_M falls inside the HI disc, so the enclosed mass there is less than M_b and r_M is an over-estimate.  r(g_bar = a_0)
      does not have this problem -- it is read off the measured curve -- which is why the two differ by ~0.1 dex in median.
    mutation a_0 x 10: N=19, r_0/r(g_bar=a_0) median 8.77 (+0.75 dex from the canonical value), scatter 0.389 dex
    mutation a_0 / 10: N=125, r_0/r(g_bar=a_0) median 0.44 (-0.55 dex from the canonical value), scatter 0.507 dex
  [PASS] M108 mutation: a_0 x 10 moves the median ratio by ~0.5 dex and changes which galaxies even have a crossing, so the number does depend on a_0 -- the item's failure is a failure of the PREDICTION, not of an a_0-blind estimator   (median ratio 1.56 -> 8.77 (+0.75 dex), N with a crossing 55 -> 19)

  VERDICT 108.  The item's premise is wrong for most of SPARC: there is no radius where g_bar = a_0 in two thirds of these
  galaxies, because they are deep-MOND everywhere, yet they still have fitted cores.  On the third where the radius exists,
  the ratio carries 0.36-0.72 dex of scatter and is a dead heat with three other length scales, two of which (R_disk, R_eff)
  contain no a_0 at all.  The framework's core-radius statement survives only in the weak form item 24 already recorded
  (r_0 tracks r_M with about half a dex of scatter); the strong form asked for here is refuted by the data on disk.
  What would make this testable: a sample selected to STRADDLE a_0 -- high-surface-brightness discs whose inner regions are
  Newtonian -- rather than SPARC, which is dominated by galaxies that never leave the deep-MOND regime.

======================================================================================================================
SUMMARY
======================================================================================================================
  106 REFUTED as posed.  There is no single slope to test: 12 profile/prior combinations on the SAME 175 curves give -0.89
       to -1.69.  The prior-free cored fits give -1.193 +- 0.109 naive and -1.328 +- 0.123 degeneracy-corrected -- STEEPER than the
       framework's -1 by 2.7 sigma, and further still from feedback's -0.7/-0.9.  The trap is real and its shape is now known:
       attenuation is a MAGNET at ~-1 (population-averaged degeneracy slope -1.07), so the naive fit reads -1 back for anything
       nearby; a pure null returns only -0.21.  A pure Dutton & Maccio c-M relation gives -0.57 with no a_0 in it at all.
       What survives is the INTERCEPT: rho_0 r_0 = 193 Msun/pc^2, +0.26 (canonical) / +0.18 (alt) dex from the two footings,
       against a 1.2 dex profile-choice systematic.  A consistency, as item 5 already recorded it; not a law.
  107 FAILS its stated condition.  c200 tracks M200 (r = -0.417) better than the disc's central surface density (r = +0.053)
       in 3 of 4 models; after the shared-error correction the c-M relation is consistent with Dutton & Maccio in all four, and
       the framework's zero-parameter c ~ M_b^(-1/4) is 5 sigma off in slope and -0.31 dex in normalisation.  ONE thing survives
       for the framework: at fixed M200 the disc's central surface density carries a 2.8 sigma positive signal that a halo-only
       c(M) relation has no reason to produce.  Upsilon-blind by construction, so it is not bug pattern 5.
  108 REFUTED as posed, and refuted at the premise.  r(g_bar = a_0) does not EXIST for 67% of SPARC: the median galaxy
       peaks at g_bar,max = 0.30 a_0 and is deep-MOND at every measured radius, yet still has a fitted core.  That finding is
       Upsilon-robust across 0.3-0.8.  On the third where the radius exists, r_0/r(g_bar=a_0) scatters 0.36 dex against a 0.2 dex
       bar and ties with three other length scales, two of which know nothing about a_0.

